Absolutely. Since you're on Windows, the easiest way is to open the SkillBridge project in VS Code + Terminal, install the required packages, and run the backend.

I recommend using the ZIP I prepared: SkillBridge_Excel_Backend.zip.

Step 1 — Download and extract the ZIP

Download the ZIP from the previous message and extract it.

After extracting, you should have a folder like:

SkillBridge_Excel_Backend
└── skillbridge
    ├── app.py
    ├── excel_service.py
    ├── firebase_service.py
    ├── matching.py
    ├── users.xlsx
    ├── skillbridge.db
    ├── requirements.txt
    ├── templates
    └── static

The important file is:

skillbridge
Step 2 — Open the project in VS Code

Open Visual Studio Code.

Then:

File → Open Folder

Select the skillbridge folder.

You should see files such as:

app.py
excel_service.py
firebase_service.py
matching.py
requirements.txt
users.xlsx
templates
static
Step 3 — Open the terminal

In VS Code, click:

Terminal → New Terminal

A terminal will appear at the bottom.

You should see something similar to:

PS C:\Users\YourName\...\skillbridge>

If you're not inside the skillbridge folder, type:

cd path\to\skillbridge

For example:

cd C:\Users\YourName\Downloads\SkillBridge_Excel_Backend\skillbridge

Then press Enter.

Step 4 — Check that Python is installed

Type:

python --version

You should get something like:

Python 3.11.5

If python doesn't work, try:

py --version
Step 5 — Create a virtual environment

Run:

python -m venv venv

Then activate it:

venv\Scripts\activate

Your terminal should now look something like:

(venv) PS C:\Users\YourName\...\skillbridge>

The (venv) is important — it means the project's virtual environment is active.

Step 6 — Install all required packages

Run:

pip install -r requirements.txt

Wait for it to finish.

You'll see packages being installed.

The Excel functionality specifically requires openpyxl, which is included in the requirements file.

Step 7 — Check the Excel database

Inside VS Code, click:

users.xlsx

You should see the users worksheet.

It contains account information such as:

id
name
email
skills
password_hash
created_at
last_seen

Don't manually change the password hash.

Step 8 — Start the SkillBridge backend

In the terminal, run:

python app.py

If everything is working, you should see something similar to:

* Running on http://127.0.0.1:5000

or:

* Running on http://localhost:5000
Step 9 — Open the website

Open Chrome.

Go to:

http://127.0.0.1:5000

You should now see your SkillBridge website.

Step 10 — Test the Excel account system
Create an account

Go to:

Register / Sign Up

Enter something like:

Name: Test User
Email: test@example.com
Skills: Python, React
Password: Test123!

Click Sign Up.

Then stop for a moment and open:

users.xlsx

You should see the new account in the Excel sheet.

Test login

Go back to the website.

Enter:

Email: test@example.com
Password: Test123!

Click Login.

The application should find the user from the Excel database and load the profile.

Step 11 — How the files work

You don't need to open every file individually to run the project.

The main flow is:

                   ┌──────────────┐
                   │   Browser    │
                   └──────┬───────┘
                          │
                          ▼
                   ┌──────────────┐
                   │    app.py    │
                   └──────┬───────┘
                          │
             ┌────────────┴────────────┐
             ▼                         ▼
      ┌──────────────┐          ┌──────────────┐
      │  users.xlsx  │          │ skillbridge  │
      │              │          │    .db       │
      │ Accounts     │          │ Chat/etc.    │
      │ Profiles     │          │ Connections  │
      │ Password hash│          │ Sessions     │
      └──────────────┘          └──────────────┘
The important code files
File	Purpose
app.py	Main Flask application
excel_service.py	Reads/writes account data to Excel
users.xlsx	Account/profile database
skillbridge.db	Existing SQLite database
firebase_service.py	Firebase-related functionality
matching.py	User matching logic
templates/	Website HTML pages
static/	CSS and JavaScript
requirements.txt	Python packages
Step 12 — If you want to see all the code from Terminal

Inside the VS Code terminal, you can list everything with:

dir

To see the HTML files:

dir templates

To see CSS/JavaScript:

dir static

To open app.py in VS Code from the terminal:

code app.py

To open the Excel service:

code excel_service.py

To open all project files in VS Code, simply use:

code .

That's usually the easiest option.

⭐ The shortest version

Once you've extracted the project and opened the skillbridge folder in VS Code:

python -m venv venv

then:

venv\Scripts\activate

then:

pip install -r requirements.txt

then:

python app.py

Then open:

http://127.0.0.1:5000

Keep the terminal running while you use the website. To stop the server, press Ctrl + C.

If you get an error at any step, copy-paste the entire red/error message from the terminal here, and I can tell you exactly what command to run next.
